00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:39.730
but I am currently-
- How do you wanna

00:20:39.730 --> 00:20:41.153
get into it?
- Laughing at them.

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:46.220
- I don't know, I could
just start with one.

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:47.053
- Sure.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:51.753
- So I kinda was thinking about things

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:55.810
that reliably make me laugh.

00:20:55.810 --> 00:20:57.310
Like, I'll be honest.

00:20:57.310 --> 00:21:02.310
I don't often make the
decision to be like,

00:21:02.996 --> 00:21:06.470
I specifically want to laugh right now.

00:21:06.470 --> 00:21:09.550
Like, that is not a-
- I really need to laugh.

00:21:09.550 --> 00:21:11.630
I'm gonna go here. Yeah, I don't...

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:12.950
- That is not a common thing.

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:13.960
- I don't think like that, either.

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:15.280
- And we've talked about
this before on the show,

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:18.340
that we typically, when we think about...

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.450
If we're gonna commit
to a television show,

00:21:20.450 --> 00:21:22.240
very rarely will it be a comedy.

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:24.720
Now, some comedies, I absolutely love,

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:27.470
but my favorite shows

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:31.210
are dramas, right?

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:32.840
Those are the things I kinda get lost in,

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:34.650
and even from movies,

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:37.220
I typically like a drama
more than I like a comedy.

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:39.077
I don't just make the choice to be like,

00:21:39.077 --> 00:21:41.690
"Hey, let's watch this because the primary

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:45.380
intention is for us to
laugh," and I don't know...

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:48.020
I've heard that from a lot
of people who are comedians,

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:51.210
that they don't find their
entertainment in comedy

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:52.440
as much as they find it in other things,

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:54.030
'cause they're spending all day

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:55.510
doing things to make people laugh

00:21:55.510 --> 00:21:56.550
or thinking about things
that make people laugh.

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:58.050
So I don't know, maybe that
has something to do with it.

00:21:58.050 --> 00:22:00.350
- Maybe there's an element of work mode.

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:02.640
I think there's also an element of, like,

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:07.350
really escaping into a story
that you can really invest in

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:08.660
and put some time into.

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:11.020
And that level of escape is akin

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:14.200
to getting a laugh from something,

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:15.523
and-
- Right.

00:22:17.349 --> 00:22:20.600
- But I do think I would
like to be a little more

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:25.190
intentional in not shying away
from seeking out laughter,

00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:27.450
because it does the body good.

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:28.283
- Yeah.

00:22:28.283 --> 00:22:31.330
- So if you were to go,
where would you go first?

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:36.330
- Well, just the other day,
we were here in the office-

00:22:36.662 --> 00:22:37.495
- (chuckles) Yeah.

00:22:37.495 --> 00:22:40.690
- Shooting some stuff, and
then we had a lunch break.

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:43.330
And sometimes we'll eat lunch

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:45.730
and not have a meeting
over lunch. (chuckles)

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:47.210
And this was one of those occasions,

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:52.020
so we could eat lunch and
just put something on the TV.

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:57.020
And I brought up, I think
the name of the video is,

00:22:57.777 --> 00:23:01.340
"Vic Berger Presents the
Very Best of Jim Bakker."

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:03.700
Now you remember, if you
watched "Good Mythical Morning"

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:05.870
a couple of years ago,
(Link laughing)

00:23:05.870 --> 00:23:08.930
Jim Bakker is the televangelist

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:12.160
who was really popular in
the '80s, he and Tammy Faye,

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:16.440
and they also, you know,
they were guilty of fraud,

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:17.900
and he took a bunch of money,

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:20.120
but he had this sort of redemption.

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:21.530
He went to prison and he came back

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:24.190
and he is, again, a televangelist.

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:27.020
And I think he's set up
in Branson, Missouri.

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:29.720
He invites these audiences
into his program.

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:33.290
He's got a new wife, who's
kinda Tammy Faye-like,

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:34.850
wears not as much makeup,

00:23:34.850 --> 00:23:36.470
but she kinda serves the same role

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:39.130
that Tammy Faye served
for him back in the day.

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:41.810
And the thing that he
has gotten obsessed with,

00:23:41.810 --> 00:23:44.680
as many televangelists
do, is the End Times.

00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:47.850
And he's not just talking
about the End Times,

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:50.660
and the apocalypse
that's always impending.

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:53.240
He's also selling things that you can

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:54.970
prepare for the End Times with,

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:56.355
including-
- And survive.

00:23:56.355 --> 00:23:58.280
- Jim Bakker Buckets,

00:23:58.280 --> 00:24:01.080
which we taste-tested on
"Good Mythical Morning."

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:02.550
You should watch that
episode if you haven't.

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:07.160
- It's like a big five-gallon
plastic paint bucket,

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:11.020
but it's filled with
food, and on his show,

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:13.690
which he's got like a morning show set

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:15.890
and his wife is a cohost,

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:17.990
and he's got these
(chuckles) other cohosts,

00:24:17.990 --> 00:24:21.930
and they've got a studio
audience that's sitting

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:24.830
in dinner place settings-
- Old folks, mostly.

00:24:24.830 --> 00:24:29.460
- Yeah, and I mean, you'd
think he would open a bucket

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:32.780
and it would just be full
of creamed corn or potatoes,

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:36.450
because they make it in the
buckets and they stir it as if

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:39.140
they're feeding the 5,000!
- They present it in the most

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:42.806
unappetizing fashion imaginable,

00:24:42.806 --> 00:24:45.300
including having a giant bucket of rice,

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:47.660
in which Jim Bakker
reaches his bare hand into

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:48.670
and just eats the rice.

00:24:48.670 --> 00:24:50.170
Now, let me just say,

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:53.610
I could watch any of this
Jim Bakker programming,

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:57.260
unedited, just as it is,
and be very entertained.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:59.310
And I have for...

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:01.390
I wanna be clear about this,

00:25:01.390 --> 00:25:05.040
because you guys know our story,

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:08.550
you know that we were raised
in conservative Christianity,

00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:10.010
evangelical Christianity,

00:25:10.010 --> 00:25:12.750
and we're no longer a
part of that tradition.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:16.240
I don't consider myself a
Christian at this point.

00:25:16.240 --> 00:25:17.290
- It feels like maybe we're coming

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:18.830
after a preacher?
- But let me just

00:25:18.830 --> 00:25:20.490
be very clear.

00:25:20.490 --> 00:25:24.490
When I was a committed

00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:26.420
evangelical Christian

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:30.100
on staff as a missionary with
a Christian organization,

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:32.370
I would still watch

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:36.633
guys like Kenneth Copeland, Jim Bakker-

00:25:37.510 --> 00:25:40.988
- TBN network, yeah.
- For entertainment purposes.

00:25:40.988 --> 00:25:43.940
Again, this is a little bit of
a deep cut, and it might be,

00:25:43.940 --> 00:25:46.510
for those of you who don't come
from a Christian background,

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:48.640
you might be like, "What,
I don't understand?"

00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:51.220
So when we were Christians,

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:55.114
we considered ourselves,
and I think rightly so,

00:25:55.114 --> 00:25:58.880
a totally separate thing from
those televangelists, right?

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:01.300
And most Christians that I
know, that I'm friends with,

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:02.950
that I was friends with,

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:04.800
that I remain friends with to this day,

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:07.850
my family, everybody, they do not...

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:09.750
They think that those dudes
are a bunch of whack jobs.

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:10.710
- Kooky.

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:13.460
- So just to their credit,

00:26:13.460 --> 00:26:16.810
they recognize that
these guys are swindlers,

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:18.510
these guys are about getting rich,

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:20.740
these guys are about taking
advantage of the vulnerable,

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:23.040
these guys are about making
these empty promises,

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:24.970
at least from the tradition
that we come from.

00:26:24.970 --> 00:26:28.670
So, but I always got such a kick out of it

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:31.480
because you can't deny
the entertainment value

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:33.150
that these televangelists bring.

00:26:33.150 --> 00:26:34.820
- Yeah, so there's no
agenda here to, like,

00:26:34.820 --> 00:26:36.530
tear some sort of preacher apart-

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:38.160
- This is something that
I was thinking about

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:40.400
exactly the same way
that I think about now,

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:44.280
my entire life, just to be clear, but-

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:47.450
- And he has taken advantage
of people by, you know,

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:51.030
tapping into fear and
selling buckets of camp food

00:26:51.030 --> 00:26:53.010
for thousands of dollars.

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:54.230
- Yeah.

00:26:54.230 --> 00:26:56.990
- Trying to sell enough of
it that he's demonstrating

00:26:56.990 --> 00:27:01.860
how you can turn the buckets
into furniture in your home.

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:04.920
- You just put a table top
over 'em, and then a curtain.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:09.650
But Vic Berger, to give the-
- V-I-C B-E-R-G-E-R.

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:11.840
- To give the other side
of this. I don't really...

00:27:11.840 --> 00:27:13.463
We've never met Vic before.

00:27:14.553 --> 00:27:17.290
I don't even know if
that's his real name, but-

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:18.123
- We love him.

00:27:18.123 --> 00:27:21.380
- He's been associated with
people like Tim and Eric,

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:26.080
and he was associated with Super Deluxe,

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:27.950
doing some stuff for them for a while.

00:27:27.950 --> 00:27:32.140
But essentially, this guy is
the master of awkward edits,

00:27:32.140 --> 00:27:34.800
of taking people who are genuinely already

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:37.630
funny and weird and awkward,

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:40.710
and then editing them in
a way that accentuates

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:44.480
that awkwardness to a complete new level.

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:47.020
The music and the edits and the way

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:48.970
that he kinda doubles back on things

00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:53.970
and emphasizes things, and-
- It's a creative layer

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:57.250
that's editorial, in a sense that, like,

00:27:57.250 --> 00:27:58.820
he's bringing his point of view to it

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:01.610
and adding another
layer, but you never lose

00:28:01.610 --> 00:28:04.620
the initial magic.
- The purity.

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:06.630
- You can watch a Vic Berger edit

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:10.660
and appreciate the fact
that, yes, it's manipulated,

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:13.940
but the things that Jim Bakker
and his cohorts are saying

00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:16.100
is not manipulated.
- I'm sure that if-

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:18.447
- He really said and did all these things,

00:28:18.447 --> 00:28:21.730
and so, like you said, you
could just watch the show.

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:23.343
You need a little more patience.

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:26.070
And you have to get over the sadness

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:28.070
of him taking advantage of people.

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:31.470
But at a certain point,
especially with Vic's editions,

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:32.800
you can just embrace it

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:36.327
as this strange comedy!

00:28:38.170 --> 00:28:42.120
- Well, I'll push back on
that getting over the sadness

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:43.897
of him taking advantage
of people, because-

00:28:43.897 --> 00:28:45.620
- "Getting over it" is not the right word.

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:47.870
- I'll just say that I
think that the people

00:28:47.870 --> 00:28:51.420
who are taken in by
people like Jim Bakker,

00:28:51.420 --> 00:28:54.160
I don't have a lot of sympathy for them.

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:55.696
- Well, (sighs) you know-
- I'm just saying-

00:28:55.696 --> 00:28:58.950
- That's harsh, because I mean,

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:01.370
you got some blue-haired
grannies, and it's just,

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:04.310
you know, you gotta protect those people.

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.137
You can't expect them-
- But hold on,

00:29:06.137 --> 00:29:08.040
but what harm is being done to them?

00:29:08.040 --> 00:29:09.140
- Money!
- They're not, I mean...

00:29:09.140 --> 00:29:11.530
- They're being drained!
- Yeah, but they're, I mean...

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:15.105
- They're getting buckets
of food. (chuckles)

00:29:15.105 --> 00:29:17.040
- I don't think people are going broke.

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:18.780
I don't think these old
folks are going broke

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:20.460
buying Jim Bakker Buckets, you know?

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:22.330
And I do think that-
- But they're scared

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:25.720
of the End Times, and they're-
- Well, and here-

00:29:25.720 --> 00:29:27.327
- There's fear-mongering-
- I do think that,

00:29:27.327 --> 00:29:29.793
and I do have, I don't
have Jim Bakker Buckets,

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:35.190
I have a fair amount, I
have a few months' supply

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:38.040
of freeze-dried food
at my house, you know?

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:40.600
And I got you some, and
that's probably the only...

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:42.731
You probably have like a couple
of weeks that I gave you.

00:29:42.731 --> 00:29:44.030
- Thank you.

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:48.550
- But it isn't like selling a blessed...

00:29:48.550 --> 00:29:49.840
And I don't know, Jim Bakker may do this,

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:52.470
but some people will sell

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:57.350
a blessed, you know, towel
that's got holy water on it.

00:29:57.350 --> 00:29:58.940
And he's not, like, healing people.

00:29:58.940 --> 00:30:00.980
He's not giving people false healing hope

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:01.813
and stuff like that.

00:30:01.813 --> 00:30:02.760
Maybe he did that back in the day.

00:30:02.760 --> 00:30:03.620
All I'm saying is,

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:05.760
I don't feel that bad
about that part of it,

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:07.870
because the worst thing that's happening

00:30:07.870 --> 00:30:11.070
is some old folks are getting
more freeze-dried food

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:12.170
than they actually will use.

00:30:12.170 --> 00:30:13.290
They're going to die,

00:30:13.290 --> 00:30:15.400
and this stuff is gonna
be on their shelves

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:17.960
and get handed down to
the next generation, so-

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:19.713
- Well, listen, but the
one thing we can agree on

00:30:19.713 --> 00:30:24.713
is that even though I feel
bad about it more so than you,

00:30:25.010 --> 00:30:27.100
I feel sorry for these people.

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:30.100
It's so funny that you
can still get over it.

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:32.080
I can still get over it,
and we can agree on that,

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:33.353
how funny it is.

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:38.780
It's taking this reality and it's adding

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.620
this just creepy juxtapositions,

00:30:41.620 --> 00:30:45.067
and you gotta watch it, but-
- And there's...

00:30:45.067 --> 00:30:46.510
And I'm sure this is one of the things

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:51.510
that you're gonna talk about, no actor...

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:56.780
I'm not interested in an actor

00:30:56.780 --> 00:30:59.293
portraying a Jim Bakker character.

00:31:00.230 --> 00:31:04.073
Now, sure, I would
laugh at it. Like, okay-

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:07.020
- I'd pay a ticket price to watch

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:08.990
Sam Rockwell in a Jared Hess-

00:31:08.990 --> 00:31:09.937
- Yeah, well, okay, I'm a fan-

00:31:09.937 --> 00:31:11.053
- Which, that already exists,

00:31:11.053 --> 00:31:11.886
by the way.
- I'm a fan

00:31:11.886 --> 00:31:13.920
of "The Righteous
Gemstones," the HBO show,

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:17.830
which is different, but you
know, I'm a fan of that.

00:31:17.830 --> 00:31:20.480
It's kind of making fun of that

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:23.270
kinda televangelist megachurch
culture in the South-

00:31:23.270 --> 00:31:24.530
- But it's a totally different thing

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:26.620
when you know this is a dramatization.

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:28.200
They're actors.
- But I would much rather

00:31:28.200 --> 00:31:30.670
see the real thing.

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:31.670
And I'm sure that...

00:31:32.750 --> 00:31:36.653
Well, first of all, I know
that Jim Bakker and his wife,

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:39.290
and other people associated with him,

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:40.710
especially some of the younger people,

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:42.100
who are some of my
favorite people to watch

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:43.670
are the younger people who
are associated with it,

00:31:43.670 --> 00:31:45.350
like onstage.
- Yeah.

00:31:45.350 --> 00:31:48.360
- I know that they know
about the Vic Berger edits.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:49.990
And you know that they're mad about them

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:52.370
because they think that
it misrepresents them,

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:54.780
and like you're saying, not
really, though. (laughs)

00:31:54.780 --> 00:31:56.820
It's like, he's taking all the things

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:58.900
that you said and you did,

00:31:58.900 --> 00:32:00.430
and he's putting them together, and yes,

00:32:00.430 --> 00:32:01.590
he's manipulating the edit

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:04.020
and if this was in a court of law,

00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:05.640
this would not be admissible evidence,

00:32:05.640 --> 00:32:09.139
but you're laughing at the
things that actually happened,

00:32:09.139 --> 00:32:12.380
in the juxtaposition of them.
- Yeah, we have this shared

00:32:12.380 --> 00:32:15.880
fascination with people being real.

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:19.403
I mean, (chuckles) you
know that as a family,

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:23.490
we've dedicated our lives
to watching "Survivor."

00:32:23.490 --> 00:32:25.660
That is like our life-
- It's the funniest show

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:27.170
on television.
(both laughing)

00:32:27.170 --> 00:32:29.830
- But you know what, one of
the things that I love about it

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:33.520
is that when you get some
really good characters,

00:32:33.520 --> 00:32:38.520
which is basically just people
who are unabashedly strange,

00:32:38.860 --> 00:32:41.750
and they can't hide it, you know,

00:32:41.750 --> 00:32:46.750
out there on the island,
your soul is bared.

00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:49.730
Your personality, like all
of your defense mechanisms,

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:51.670
everything you do, it's just...

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:56.290
I love the psychological analysis of that.

00:32:56.290 --> 00:32:58.700
And some people are really funny

00:32:58.700 --> 00:33:01.790
and they do a good job,
an Emmy-winning job,

00:33:01.790 --> 00:33:03.930
of editing these characters.

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:08.430
And especially because
they're put in situations

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:11.030
that aren't romantic.

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:13.200
That's actually, you know,

00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:14.700
there's so much of that out there.

00:33:14.700 --> 00:33:17.440
That's why I love "Survivor,"
is that you're seeing

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:20.803
real people, and sometimes
really strange people,

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:23.750
being pushed to their limits,

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:27.150
and it's not about
making out with somebody.

00:33:27.150 --> 00:33:30.500
Now, I know that that's
also entertaining for you,

00:33:30.500 --> 00:33:32.850
and you really get into it.
- That, but, well,

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:38.090
I'm not into "90 Day Fiance"

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:39.890
because of the making out, trust me,

00:33:41.100 --> 00:33:43.251
or "The Bachelor," for that-

00:33:43.251 --> 00:33:46.480
- It's for the comedy.
- It's for the putting

00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:50.220
normal people in a situation...

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:52.423
In an abnormal situation, really.

00:33:53.510 --> 00:33:55.020
For me, it's the same principle.

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:57.100
I also enjoy, I enjoy "Survivor."

00:33:57.100 --> 00:33:59.630
We don't watch it
religiously, but yeah, I-

00:33:59.630 --> 00:34:01.580
- It's not a comedy, but...

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:05.020
- Yeah, but I enjoy the trashy

00:34:05.020 --> 00:34:08.750
reality shows more because the people

00:34:08.750 --> 00:34:11.910
are more extreme.

00:34:11.910 --> 00:34:13.820
Like, that's the thing. Jim Bakker...

00:34:16.146 --> 00:34:18.580
I have complicated feelings
towards Jim Bakker,

00:34:18.580 --> 00:34:22.810
because he creeps me out in a certain way,

00:34:22.810 --> 00:34:24.820
but at the same time,

00:34:24.820 --> 00:34:28.260
something about the way
(laughs) his eyes move

00:34:28.260 --> 00:34:31.210
and his mouth, the position that he has,

00:34:31.210 --> 00:34:32.880
and the way that he expresses things,

00:34:32.880 --> 00:34:34.700
is incredibly endearing.

00:34:34.700 --> 00:34:37.368
- These are things that
just happen on his face.

00:34:37.368 --> 00:34:38.950
- (laughs) Yeah!
- He hasn't...

00:34:38.950 --> 00:34:42.080
You can tell that he
hasn't watched himself back

00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:44.490
and become a persona.

00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:47.170
A lot of, I think, televangelists,

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:49.527
I think they do that;
there's a performance as...

00:34:49.527 --> 00:34:51.682
And of course, he's a
performer too, he's a host,

00:34:51.682 --> 00:34:53.020
he's been on television-

00:34:53.020 --> 00:34:55.020
- But he's not polished or slick.

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:57.640
- Yeah, but so much of
it is still instinctive.

00:34:57.640 --> 00:35:00.210
Like, why is his face
doing that right now?

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:02.580
- Right, and if he was your granddad-

00:35:02.580 --> 00:35:03.970
- It's the granddad energy.

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:05.920
- You would find all those weird things,

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:08.470
like his fashion sense,
like the fact that he...

00:35:08.470 --> 00:35:11.040
He's got that hat with the cross on it,

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:14.000
and then he's got these
vests that he'll wear

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:15.490
that look like he's going fly fishing,

00:35:15.490 --> 00:35:17.240
but he's not, he's just hosting a show.

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:18.750
These little choices

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:20.235
that he's making-
- Lotta empty pockets.

00:35:20.235 --> 00:35:24.150
- I just, I find it intoxicating. (laughs)

00:35:24.150 --> 00:35:25.160
You know what I'm saying?

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:25.993
- It's great.

00:35:27.416 --> 00:35:31.277
- And the process of
someone trying to be funny,

00:35:31.277 --> 00:35:33.580
and again, this goes back to why,

00:35:33.580 --> 00:35:36.620
when we made the local
commercials, we were so into it,

00:35:36.620 --> 00:35:39.480
'cause you take like Goorgon, you know,

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:41.323
from the "Shift It, Shift It" commercial,

00:35:41.323 --> 00:35:45.210
and the entertainment quality for us

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:49.650
was based in getting Goorgon
to make a commercial.

00:35:49.650 --> 00:35:50.550
You know what I'm saying? It was like-

00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:51.920
- Well, and it was also very...

00:35:51.920 --> 00:35:53.570
Well, what do you mean...

00:35:53.570 --> 00:35:56.210
Well, it was very
important that we preserve

00:35:56.210 --> 00:35:59.580
and platform the strangest
parts of our clients

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:01.077
that were actually real.

00:36:01.077 --> 00:36:03.140
- Well, but also-
- We didn't manufacture

00:36:03.140 --> 00:36:05.470
a personality for them.
- Like the scene where he

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:08.660
is answering the phone, but he hesitated,

00:36:08.660 --> 00:36:10.010
and he-
- Yeah.

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:12.150
- We didn't fully direct him.

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:13.470
When you wanna get these type

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:14.560
of performances out of people,

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:18.090
you direct them enough to
push them in a direction-

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:18.990
- To see what they would do.

00:36:18.990 --> 00:36:21.270
- But then you let them direct themselves.

00:36:21.270 --> 00:36:22.290
So it was like, he... (laughs)

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:24.600
I can't remember, I think
he picks up the phone

00:36:24.600 --> 00:36:27.960
and says something, and then-
- No, he's on the phone,

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:29.460
and he sees the woman drive up.

00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:30.410
- Oh yeah, but he didn't know

00:36:30.410 --> 00:36:32.350
if he should keep the
phone up or put it down.

00:36:32.350 --> 00:36:34.204
And there's like hesitation.
- (laughs) Yeah.

00:36:34.204 --> 00:36:35.377
- And it's like, we see that kinda thing,

00:36:35.377 --> 00:36:37.230
and we're like, that's the gold.

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:39.070
That's what we want. I don't know...

00:36:39.070 --> 00:36:42.930
And I can't really express, I
can't articulate what it is,

00:36:42.930 --> 00:36:43.950
but for me-
- Honest.

00:36:43.950 --> 00:36:47.560
- There's this, yeah, relatable
honesty in that moment,

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:48.590
of, he's like, "I don't know what to do

00:36:48.590 --> 00:36:50.330
with the phone."
(Link laughing)

00:36:50.330 --> 00:36:51.163
And we're like, "Well,

00:36:51.163 --> 00:36:52.660
that's why this is going
in the commercial."

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:55.470
- And it kinda worked from
a story standpoint, too,

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:58.470
that, like, I know, he
sees this beautiful woman,

00:36:58.470 --> 00:37:02.120
who just responded to a
Craigslist ad, (laughs)

00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:04.733
to show up and be in his commercial.

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:09.230
But, like, oh, you know,
he's bumfuzzled. (laughs)

00:37:09.230 --> 00:37:10.230
On a couple of...

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:11.970
That's what it seems like from a character

00:37:11.970 --> 00:37:14.040
standpoint he's doing, but
you know, at the same time,

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:16.560
that he's not an actor,
and he's doing this...

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:20.030
I mean, I know you wanna talk
about ""Family Feud," right?

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:21.950
There's a connection to that!

00:37:21.950 --> 00:37:24.230
- Yeah, so another thing that I'll do

00:37:24.230 --> 00:37:28.150
is watch compilations

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:29.440
of "Family Feud" stuff-
- Yeah,

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:31.070
we've done this for years.

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:33.253
- But more recently,

00:37:34.870 --> 00:37:38.460
I've been watching just whole
episodes of the "Family Feud."

00:37:38.460 --> 00:37:41.280
Now, is it as good as the compilations?

00:37:41.280 --> 00:37:43.290
It's not as concentrated, by any means.

00:37:43.290 --> 00:37:45.030
- Sure.
- But you actually get

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:48.770
into the gameplay, and my
family and I will sit down.

00:37:48.770 --> 00:37:49.990
You guys are watching "Survivor."

00:37:49.990 --> 00:37:51.910
The McLaughlins are
watching "Family Feud."

00:37:51.910 --> 00:37:56.200
And being committed to the gameplay,

00:37:56.200 --> 00:37:58.160
like really, everyone's trying to answer.

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:02.230
Everybody's saying the answer,

00:38:02.230 --> 00:38:03.063
and we're getting into it,

00:38:03.063 --> 00:38:05.520
and then I find myself
doing this same thing.

00:38:05.520 --> 00:38:08.250
So one of the classic
things in "Family Feud" is,

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:10.230
after the round has been completed

00:38:10.230 --> 00:38:13.253
and there's still some
unrevealed answers on the board,

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:18.730
they reveal them, and then the crowd just-

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:21.490
- Reads out loud.
- Impulsively repeats it.

00:38:21.490 --> 00:38:23.637
You know, it's like, we asked 100 people,

00:38:23.637 --> 00:38:26.187
"What's the first thing
you put on in the morning?"

00:38:27.517 --> 00:38:30.510
"Underwear!" Everybody says that.

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:34.420
And I find myself watching
it and saying, "Underwear!"

00:38:34.420 --> 00:38:36.670
- You speak out loud?

00:38:36.670 --> 00:38:37.940
- Yes, I am a-
- Wow.

00:38:37.940 --> 00:38:39.250
- I am a committed audience member,

00:38:39.250 --> 00:38:41.060
and then I'm like-
- Yeah, you are.

00:38:41.060 --> 00:38:44.300
- Okay, what is Steve
gonna do? Steve's gonna...

00:38:44.300 --> 00:38:46.070
Okay, oh, he's all over this one.

00:38:46.070 --> 00:38:48.020
They're gonna say this...

00:38:48.020 --> 00:38:49.370
He's gonna make fun of this guy.

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:50.600
Oh, he's gonna pull back a little bit

00:38:50.600 --> 00:38:52.430
'cause this girl's not
responding that well to this.

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:56.690
And also, the producers
making the decision,

00:38:56.690 --> 00:39:00.090
the whole system of "Family
Feud" is now designed

00:39:00.090 --> 00:39:02.190
to create YouTube clips.

00:39:02.190 --> 00:39:04.523
So it's gotten way more suggestive.

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:07.550
Because it used to be
accidentally suggestive,

00:39:07.550 --> 00:39:09.230
and then they were just like,
"Let's just go all the way,

00:39:09.230 --> 00:39:11.500
and let's just ask questions."

00:39:11.500 --> 00:39:12.446
Sometimes they can go-
- And they can get

00:39:12.446 --> 00:39:13.279
embarrassing answers.
- A little too far

00:39:13.279 --> 00:39:17.270
and it seems too forced,
but again, it's also,

00:39:17.270 --> 00:39:21.050
I'm fascinated by, these
people playing this game

00:39:21.050 --> 00:39:22.820
have never been on television.

00:39:22.820 --> 00:39:25.540
And I'm thinking about what
is going through this...

00:39:25.540 --> 00:39:27.440
Like, this dad brings his family,

00:39:27.440 --> 00:39:29.860
and he's thinking 17 things.

00:39:29.860 --> 00:39:32.984
He's thinking, I'm on television,
I don't wanna be an idiot.

00:39:32.984 --> 00:39:36.580
I might end up being on
YouTube. I wanna win the money.

00:39:36.580 --> 00:39:37.940
I wanna beat that other family.

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:39.920
I gotta think about the
actual way that people

00:39:39.920 --> 00:39:41.040
would've answered this.

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:42.390
- Yeah, it's tough.
- And I'm fascinated

00:39:42.390 --> 00:39:44.250
by the honest human psychology

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:45.280
of somebody being on a game show!

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:47.720
- Yeah, and it's a brilliant setup,

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:50.770
because once you have all of
that going through your mind,

00:39:50.770 --> 00:39:54.550
and then Steve Harvey's in your
face asking you a question,

00:39:54.550 --> 00:39:57.460
that surreal experience-
- You're like, I'm there now.

00:39:57.460 --> 00:39:58.420
- Then you...

00:40:01.176 --> 00:40:02.990
You can't manufacture responses!

00:40:02.990 --> 00:40:04.280
You end up just saying

00:40:04.280 --> 00:40:05.650
the first thing that comes to your mind,

00:40:05.650 --> 00:40:07.070
you know, you're playing the game,

00:40:07.070 --> 00:40:10.550
but, like, you're playing
it in this kinda weakened,

00:40:10.550 --> 00:40:13.700
laid-bare state, where
it's like, you can't...

00:40:14.850 --> 00:40:18.690
There's the self-preservation, you know,

00:40:18.690 --> 00:40:22.023
that quotient has gone
to nil by this point.

00:40:23.528 --> 00:40:25.380
And it's the perfect recipe

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:28.990
for people to embarrass
themselves with honesty.

00:40:28.990 --> 00:40:31.890
Honesty! I think that's-
- Yeah, it's great.

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:32.920
- That's what we go back to.

00:40:32.920 --> 00:40:36.700
- And you've got somebody, Steve Harvey,

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:38.850
the reason that he's such a good host

00:40:38.850 --> 00:40:43.270
is because he's incredibly honest.

00:40:43.270 --> 00:40:47.440
So, like, you can't say
something that's a dumb answer,

00:40:47.440 --> 00:40:51.037
and not have him point it out,
and not have him drag you.

00:40:51.037 --> 00:40:52.277
And some people might be like,

00:40:52.277 --> 00:40:53.440
"Well, that's kind of unfair," it's like,

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:54.960
you know what you're getting into!

00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:55.970
You know, chill out a little bit.

00:40:55.970 --> 00:40:58.120
You know what you're getting
into when you go on that show.

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:01.790
And the thing is, is that in the same...

00:41:01.790 --> 00:41:06.140
You know how you and I, we
make observations about people.

00:41:06.140 --> 00:41:07.790
We've talked about this many times,

00:41:07.790 --> 00:41:09.440
where we'll go to a party, whatever,

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:10.800
we have an interaction with somebody,

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:14.040
and there'll be something
specific about a person,

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:15.950
just something notable about them.

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:18.820
And then we'll get into the car, go home,

00:41:18.820 --> 00:41:21.200
and be like, "Hey, when
we talked to that guy,

00:41:21.200 --> 00:41:24.180
what did you notice about
him?" and then we'll be like...

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:25.370
We have fun figuring out, well,

00:41:25.370 --> 00:41:27.190
what was it that you noticed about him?

00:41:27.190 --> 00:41:31.500
It was his jacket, or it was the way

00:41:31.500 --> 00:41:34.240
he pointed at somebody
when he talking to them,

00:41:34.240 --> 00:41:36.250
you know, some specific thing.

00:41:36.250 --> 00:41:37.790
We're fascinated by that element

00:41:37.790 --> 00:41:39.007
of human behavior.
- Was he winking?

00:41:39.007 --> 00:41:40.740
Or was there something in his eye?

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:44.160
- And Steve Harvey will not let
you get away with something.

00:41:44.160 --> 00:41:46.880
He points it out and
then he can (chuckles)

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:49.410
be funny about it in the moment,
that's what makes him good.

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:51.437
Every "Family Feud" host
has been able to do that.

00:41:51.437 --> 00:41:53.680
- But the observational sense of humor

00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:55.517
does make me think of "Seinfeld."

00:41:56.688 --> 00:42:00.163
That was our favorite sitcom growing up.

00:42:01.614 --> 00:42:04.620
It was unlike any sitcom before it.

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:06.960
But it's taking...

00:42:08.290 --> 00:42:11.290
You could tell when you were
watching these characters,

00:42:11.290 --> 00:42:12.380
and everything that happened,

00:42:12.380 --> 00:42:16.890
that it was Larry David and Jerry Seinfeld

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:20.270
pumping these characters full
of their observational humor.

00:42:20.270 --> 00:42:25.270
So yeah, you're one step removed
in that these are actors,

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:30.090
but the subject matter was
still that observational honesty

00:42:30.090 --> 00:42:32.423
and exploring that stuff, and then,

00:42:34.220 --> 00:42:37.763
with a character like Kramer,
or a lot of the characters,

00:42:39.240 --> 00:42:42.690
yeah, it was the other characters
kinda picking them apart,

00:42:42.690 --> 00:42:44.603
Jerry picking apart his dates,

00:42:45.780 --> 00:42:48.970
then everyone picking
apart Elaine's dates,

00:42:48.970 --> 00:42:51.150
including Elaine, you know?

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:54.360
And, well, they picked apart everybody

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:56.090
that was outside of their circle,

00:42:56.090 --> 00:42:58.960
which I think is something
that maybe we enjoyed

00:42:58.960 --> 00:43:01.410
and then adopted in our friendship,

00:43:01.410 --> 00:43:02.300
in sort of picking apart other things.

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.030
- But they were all assholes.

00:43:04.030 --> 00:43:04.863
- Yeah.

00:43:04.863 --> 00:43:06.900
- All of 'em, all four of the main cast.

00:43:06.900 --> 00:43:08.680
- Who never changed.
- Well, Kramer wasn't-

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:10.850
- No, he wasn't, yeah.
- As much, but,

00:43:10.850 --> 00:43:13.530
they were all assholes, but...

00:43:13.530 --> 00:43:15.450
And that's one of the
reasons people didn't think

00:43:15.450 --> 00:43:19.978
that it would work, it's like,
well, who likes these people?

00:43:19.978 --> 00:43:21.830
And it also might be
one of the reasons that-

00:43:21.830 --> 00:43:23.220
- But I didn't like-
- The younger generation

00:43:23.220 --> 00:43:24.770
watches it now and it's
like, well, first of all,

00:43:24.770 --> 00:43:26.357
you have to get over the '90s of it,

00:43:26.357 --> 00:43:28.113
and the laugh track and all that stuff,

00:43:28.113 --> 00:43:28.990
that might get in the way.
- But it wasn't that

00:43:28.990 --> 00:43:32.550
I liked them first; I
understood how they thought.

00:43:32.550 --> 00:43:33.383
- Right. Yeah, exactly!

00:43:33.383 --> 00:43:36.120
- And then you start
to like them as people

00:43:36.120 --> 00:43:37.872
when you start to appreciate more of it.

00:43:37.872 --> 00:43:41.210
But, you know, I was thinking about, like,

00:43:41.210 --> 00:43:43.350
what are the shows I'll watch now?

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:46.240
I watched "Ted Lasso"
and I really liked it.

00:43:46.240 --> 00:43:48.799
- But not necessarily for the comedy.

00:43:48.799 --> 00:43:51.000
I mean there were
definitely funny moments.

00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:52.520
I liked it for the heart.

00:43:52.520 --> 00:43:55.420
- Yeah, for the heart, I
think it was, you know,

00:43:55.420 --> 00:43:58.860
I really enjoyed how the characters

00:43:58.860 --> 00:44:01.760
all went on journeys, and yeah,

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:04.390
I don't think it was first
and foremost the comedy

00:44:04.390 --> 00:44:06.150
that made me love the show.

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:07.380
It took a while to get into it.

00:44:07.380 --> 00:44:09.680
There was like some hokey
situations or whatever, but-

00:44:09.680 --> 00:44:11.780
- Some of my least favorite
parts of "Ted Lasso"

00:44:11.780 --> 00:44:13.270
are some of the jokes that don't work.

00:44:13.270 --> 00:44:16.564
- Now, I think, I think
Ted Lasso as a character-

00:44:16.564 --> 00:44:18.121
- Oh, yeah.
- Like, he worked.

00:44:18.121 --> 00:44:19.700
He was locked in.
- I loved the show.

00:44:19.700 --> 00:44:20.710
- He was locked in.

00:44:20.710 --> 00:44:22.850
It was the people around
him and the type of jokes.

00:44:22.850 --> 00:44:25.670
But, you know, as we've talked

00:44:25.670 --> 00:44:27.840
about the things that we like the most,

00:44:27.840 --> 00:44:30.520
like when I think of television,
I think of "Seinfeld,"

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:34.520
I think of "The Office," and
then when I think of movies,

00:44:34.520 --> 00:44:37.440
and I try to draw some
conclusions from this,

00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:40.020
the type of comedy movies that we like...

00:44:41.480 --> 00:44:46.160
The Adam Sandler movies,
even the Will Ferrell movies,

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:49.113
that type of stuff that
was like over the top,

00:44:50.860 --> 00:44:53.540
I watched some of those, but
I never really got into it.

00:44:53.540 --> 00:44:55.470
The type of comedies
that I really got into

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:57.540
were like the Coen brothers comedies,

00:44:57.540 --> 00:44:59.300
and then trying to figure out,

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:01.640
why is it that I thought
"The Big Lebowski"

00:45:01.640 --> 00:45:05.270
was so much funnier than, well,

00:45:05.270 --> 00:45:07.410
I was gonna say "Talladega Nights,"

00:45:07.410 --> 00:45:09.706
but that's a really funny movie.

00:45:09.706 --> 00:45:11.740
- I think, well-
- That one's an exception

00:45:11.740 --> 00:45:12.573
because it-
- I also think

00:45:12.573 --> 00:45:15.750
that Will Ferrell and Adam Sandler,

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:18.380
it's a very different approach.

00:45:18.380 --> 00:45:21.570
- Yeah, once I began to appreciate
the Adam McKay of it all,

00:45:21.570 --> 00:45:22.988
and once he started to create

00:45:22.988 --> 00:45:26.800
so many other Oscar-worthy movies,

00:45:26.800 --> 00:45:29.707
is that, like, I started to reassess

00:45:29.707 --> 00:45:33.510
how I'd written off those
Will Ferrell movies.

00:45:33.510 --> 00:45:35.050
And I do think that they're great.

00:45:35.050 --> 00:45:37.790
But I think that the
thing that I was drawn to,

00:45:37.790 --> 00:45:40.740
and I think both of us, and
that we've talked about,

00:45:40.740 --> 00:45:43.800
is, well, we definitely don't like

00:45:43.800 --> 00:45:45.640
these joke-oriented sitcoms.

00:45:45.640 --> 00:45:48.783
We like more of the
situational-oriented sitcoms,

00:45:50.060 --> 00:45:53.830
and have that different single-cam tone,

00:45:53.830 --> 00:45:56.400
versus the multi-cam "Big Bang Theory,"

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:59.000
like, every word out of
their mouth is a joke,

00:45:59.000 --> 00:46:01.580
and everything is just a setup

00:46:01.580 --> 00:46:04.390
for them to get to tell more jokes.

00:46:04.390 --> 00:46:07.690
And realizing that
didn't resonate with me,

00:46:07.690 --> 00:46:09.577
but seeing what was different
about the things that did,

00:46:09.577 --> 00:46:12.113
and I think one of the
things that I concluded was,

00:46:14.100 --> 00:46:17.060
I mean, having characters

00:46:17.060 --> 00:46:21.120
that are built funny that I could...

00:46:21.120 --> 00:46:23.026
I think that there's something to that

00:46:23.026 --> 00:46:24.530
in what we've already talked about,

00:46:24.530 --> 00:46:27.940
about our fascination with
the comedy and real people.

00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:30.853
Like a really well-built
comedic character,

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:33.690
take any character from
"The Big Lebowski."

00:46:33.690 --> 00:46:34.700
I think it applies.

00:46:34.700 --> 00:46:36.430
And then you put those type of characters

00:46:36.430 --> 00:46:40.940
in situations that are
funny and then let them say

00:46:40.940 --> 00:46:43.583
what they would say and do and
respond in those situations,

00:46:45.271 --> 00:46:47.133
and that's-
- And it will be funny.

00:46:47.133 --> 00:46:48.698
- And it will be funny.

00:46:48.698 --> 00:46:52.370
I love that, as opposed to
the joke-oriented stuff,

00:46:52.370 --> 00:46:54.160
when it's like, all of
this is just a setup

00:46:54.160 --> 00:46:56.310
so they can say things that are funny,

00:46:56.310 --> 00:46:58.353
and then it's all about, well,

00:46:59.460 --> 00:47:01.160
did I laugh at that joke or not?

00:47:01.160 --> 00:47:03.420
And did I laugh at a high
enough percentage of the jokes

00:47:03.420 --> 00:47:04.770
to think this movie was good?

00:47:04.770 --> 00:47:06.270
- I think it's when you can sense

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:10.510
the presence of the writer,
it's a turnoff, right?

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:11.990
- For us, yeah.

00:47:11.990 --> 00:47:16.990
- Yeah, and when you say
funny situations versus jokes-

00:47:18.066 --> 00:47:19.270
- When we first talked about that,

00:47:19.270 --> 00:47:21.610
we thought it was like a revelation.

00:47:21.610 --> 00:47:23.673
It seems pretty obvious.
- No, but I think the way

00:47:23.673 --> 00:47:26.343
that you broke it down is exactly right.

00:47:27.300 --> 00:47:30.360
It's funny people being
put in funny situations,

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:34.463
and they will say memorable, funny things,

00:47:34.463 --> 00:47:39.340
but it's not jokes, and it might...

00:47:39.340 --> 00:47:40.390
Okay, so you take something...

00:47:40.390 --> 00:47:42.440
One of my favorite shows, "Barry," right?

00:47:44.397 --> 00:47:49.397
"Barry" on HBO, Bill
Hader plays, basically,

00:47:49.420 --> 00:47:51.440
a hitman who's trying to-

00:47:51.440 --> 00:47:52.820
- Become an actor.
- And it has one of our

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:54.620
favorite actors in it, Stephen Root.

00:48:00.276 --> 00:48:02.048
It kinda toes the line, right?

00:48:02.048 --> 00:48:05.470
There are some ridiculous characters

00:48:05.470 --> 00:48:09.210
that say things that no one
would ever actually say,

00:48:09.210 --> 00:48:11.920
no matter who they were, you
put them in this situation,

00:48:11.920 --> 00:48:15.020
okay this, that was a writer's idea

00:48:15.020 --> 00:48:16.160
of what would be a funny thing to say.

00:48:16.160 --> 00:48:18.770
That was a joke; that was a
setup, that was a punchline.

00:48:18.770 --> 00:48:22.970
It happens, and you know what,

00:48:22.970 --> 00:48:26.900
when they go a little bit
too far, I don't like it,

00:48:26.900 --> 00:48:29.760
but I love the show
because they don't do it

00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:31.650
very often-
- Oh, the way we put it

00:48:31.650 --> 00:48:33.660
was choices-
- Yeah, funny choices

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:36.219
versus jokes.
- Yes. It's like, so...

00:48:36.219 --> 00:48:38.900
It's kinda like behind the scenes with...

00:48:38.900 --> 00:48:41.623
The brilliance of
"Barry" is that you take,

00:48:42.550 --> 00:48:43.560
you build these characters,

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:47.790
you put 'em in this high-stakes situation,

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:51.140
but you tweak everything for the comedy.

00:48:51.140 --> 00:48:52.970
Everything that, it could be, I mean,

00:48:52.970 --> 00:48:55.770
this could be Kiefer Sutherland in "24."

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:58.200
You can have the same
characters in the same story,

00:48:58.200 --> 00:49:00.860
but the way that all the situations

00:49:00.860 --> 00:49:05.860
are turned towards comedy to
kinda skewer the acting world

00:49:05.880 --> 00:49:08.525
and acting classes and
just the situations,

00:49:08.525 --> 00:49:13.260
the irony of somebody who is a killer

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:14.773
trying to become an actor.

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:19.090
We just can sense those funny...

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:22.897
The choices set up the comedy, not the...

00:49:22.897 --> 00:49:27.897
And then you trust the payoff,
versus writing the joke,

00:49:28.300 --> 00:49:30.110
the punchline as a payoff.
- And in the stuff

00:49:30.110 --> 00:49:32.250
that we're currently working on,

00:49:32.250 --> 00:49:35.380
which we continue to
develop and write things

00:49:35.380 --> 00:49:37.796
that we really hope
you'll see at some point,

00:49:37.796 --> 00:49:39.660
but that's really what we're focusing on.

00:49:39.660 --> 00:49:40.740
Now, I don't-
- it's so important,

00:49:40.740 --> 00:49:43.030
the beginning is so important
to us for those reasons,

00:49:43.030 --> 00:49:45.680
'cause then you trust that
if you set it up right,

00:49:45.680 --> 00:49:48.100
that the comedy will take care of itself.

00:49:48.100 --> 00:49:49.700
- And let me just be clear,

00:49:49.700 --> 00:49:52.047
this is not what we did on "Buddy System."

00:49:53.518 --> 00:49:56.610
Season one, which we always sorta

00:49:56.610 --> 00:49:58.783
dog a little bit and say isn't any good,

00:50:00.913 --> 00:50:02.063
(sighs) it's not great.

00:50:03.340 --> 00:50:07.800
It had some jokes in it that didn't land.

00:50:07.800 --> 00:50:10.090
We kinda did the funny situation thing.

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:11.850
Second season, we like better

00:50:11.850 --> 00:50:14.940
because it was better executed,
it was a better story,

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:16.100
we were playing characters.

00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:18.090
We weren't just playing ourselves.

00:50:18.090 --> 00:50:22.830
But it was absurdist,
and also, it did have...

00:50:22.830 --> 00:50:24.680
Many things that were
said and done were things

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:26.010
that no one would ever say and do.

00:50:26.010 --> 00:50:28.700
And I think that it worked and
I do like absurdist comedy,

00:50:28.700 --> 00:50:32.150
and I even like sitcoms at times.

00:50:32.150 --> 00:50:33.900
But none of the stuff that we're currently

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:36.340
working on plays in that world.

00:50:36.340 --> 00:50:39.070
Right now, everything that
we're working on is like,

00:50:39.070 --> 00:50:41.270
these characters are funny.

00:50:41.270 --> 00:50:43.670
This situation that we're putting them in-

00:50:43.670 --> 00:50:45.380
- Is dramatic.
- Is going to draw

00:50:45.380 --> 00:50:48.770
out the funny, because as
they navigate this world,

00:50:48.770 --> 00:50:51.860
or this serious situation
or this perilous situation,

00:50:51.860 --> 00:50:54.440
they're gonna end up saying
and doing funny things,

00:50:54.440 --> 00:50:56.237
because that's the way
the characters are built,

00:50:56.237 --> 00:50:59.220
but we're gonna try to authentically

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:01.190
represent what we think this character

00:51:01.190 --> 00:51:03.230
would actually do in this situation.

00:51:03.230 --> 00:51:07.790
And that's kind of a big shift
for us creatively, right?

00:51:07.790 --> 00:51:10.260
Because again, when you
think about what we do

00:51:10.260 --> 00:51:12.330
and the sort of sketches that we put out

00:51:12.330 --> 00:51:16.510
on Instagram or TikTok
or that kinda thing,

00:51:16.510 --> 00:51:19.360
it's like, those are, it's a
completely different medium.

00:51:21.860 --> 00:51:23.980
The conceit is, we're in a comical,

00:51:23.980 --> 00:51:27.840
ridiculous, nonbelievable world.

00:51:27.840 --> 00:51:30.010
So, and it's a totally different thing.

00:51:30.010 --> 00:51:32.130
The stuff that we're
working on narratively,

00:51:32.130 --> 00:51:33.460
like longer-form stuff,

00:51:33.460 --> 00:51:35.880
is in that sort of more authentic place.

00:51:35.880 --> 00:51:38.620
- But let's go to the
social media of it all,

00:51:38.620 --> 00:51:39.760
because, I mean...

00:51:40.900 --> 00:51:43.920
I mean, of course my
kids only go to TikTok,

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:47.210
and so much of that is comedy, you know?

00:51:47.210 --> 00:51:49.202
And they're just sitting there laughing

00:51:49.202 --> 00:51:52.120
and sharing stuff with each other.

00:51:52.120 --> 00:51:54.617
- And I think the reason
that, I'll show...

00:51:54.617 --> 00:51:57.540
And I know you know the guy
that I'm gonna talk about,

00:51:57.540 --> 00:51:59.790
'cause you think he's funny as well,

00:51:59.790 --> 00:52:04.400
I think the thing that
makes a funny TikTok for me,

00:52:04.400 --> 00:52:06.320
and again, this is obviously
completely scripted,

00:52:06.320 --> 00:52:08.690
because in the example that
we're gonna talk about,

00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:13.490
in many examples, it's one
person playing every single role,

00:52:13.490 --> 00:52:16.840
so it's obviously fake
and obviously written.

00:52:16.840 --> 00:52:18.033
It's a narrative thing.

00:52:19.240 --> 00:52:22.850
It's the very specific
choices that make it

00:52:22.850 --> 00:52:25.640
feel like fresh comedy.

00:52:25.640 --> 00:52:28.060
It makes it feel like
somebody is kinda pushing

00:52:28.060 --> 00:52:30.950
into a new sort of boundary

00:52:30.950 --> 00:52:33.100
and a new way of thinking about comedy,

00:52:33.100 --> 00:52:34.810
or there's just something
about this person

00:52:34.810 --> 00:52:36.640
that is just intrinsically funny.

00:52:36.640 --> 00:52:39.200
So I'm gonna talk about

00:52:43.420 --> 00:52:46.390
Petey USA.
(Link chuckles)

00:52:46.390 --> 00:52:50.793
So P-E-T-E-Y U-S-A,

00:52:52.070 --> 00:52:53.800
long-haired, bearded guy.

00:52:53.800 --> 00:52:54.744
I mean, he's only got-
- How many followers

00:52:54.744 --> 00:52:58.500
does he got?
- 585,000 followers

00:52:58.500 --> 00:53:01.213
at the time of this recording.

00:53:02.090 --> 00:53:05.810
And listen, I don't know
anything about this guy.

00:53:05.810 --> 00:53:07.257
All I know is his TikToks,

00:53:07.257 --> 00:53:09.760
and I find his TikToks very funny.

00:53:09.760 --> 00:53:11.380
So I'm just gonna, let's just...

00:53:11.380 --> 00:53:13.130
Did you see the one about cornhole?

00:53:13.980 --> 00:53:17.230
- Yeah. I've seen a lot of
these. I really like him.

00:53:17.230 --> 00:53:18.710
- Okay, well, let's just
watch the one about cornhole.

00:53:18.710 --> 00:53:21.220
- And I mean, he's playing this...

00:53:21.220 --> 00:53:23.220
Petey USA is this character,

00:53:23.220 --> 00:53:25.080
but something about the fact

00:53:25.930 --> 00:53:27.190
that you don't know what you're gonna get

00:53:27.190 --> 00:53:28.760
when you scroll across TikTok.

00:53:28.760 --> 00:53:31.070
You wanna believe that
this is a real person.

00:53:31.070 --> 00:53:33.640
He does such a good job in his portrayal,

00:53:33.640 --> 00:53:38.093
that, I mean, the nature of
the comedy is so strange,

00:53:38.960 --> 00:53:43.960
that you know that it's intentional,

00:53:45.550 --> 00:53:47.680
but it's also so strange
that it must've come

00:53:47.680 --> 00:53:48.800
from a really strange person,

00:53:48.800 --> 00:53:51.130
so you start to think that
this guy's self-aware enough

00:53:51.130 --> 00:53:53.720
to tap into his strangeness.

00:53:53.720 --> 00:53:56.698
But I think there's a lot of the real guy-

00:53:56.698 --> 00:53:58.450
- Definitely! Definitely.
- Out front here.

00:53:58.450 --> 00:54:02.010
So even that, trying to
parse that, is fascinating.

00:54:02.010 --> 00:54:04.090
- He's not trying to be
somebody who he's not.

00:54:04.090 --> 00:54:06.500
He's trying to be an
elevated version of himself.

00:54:06.500 --> 00:54:09.130
Okay, let's watch this one about cornhole.

00:54:09.130 --> 00:54:09.963
- Mkay.
(cornbag thuds)

00:54:09.963 --> 00:54:11.920
Playing cornhole.
- Dammit.

00:54:11.920 --> 00:54:14.440
- Oh, a hand comes out of the cornhole,

00:54:14.440 --> 00:54:17.020
takes his corn bag-
- What the fuck?

00:54:17.020 --> 00:54:18.710
(quiet music)
- It's kinda creepy.

00:54:18.710 --> 00:54:20.040
- Hey, man!
- Oh, it's him,

00:54:20.040 --> 00:54:20.873
in the cornhole.
- How'd you get in there?

00:54:20.873 --> 00:54:22.489
I'm cut in half, man.

00:54:22.489 --> 00:54:23.905
Oh my god!
- He's cut in half.

00:54:23.905 --> 00:54:24.738
- How are you not even bleeding?

00:54:24.738 --> 00:54:26.696
Oh, I'm bleeding pretty good, actually.

00:54:26.696 --> 00:54:29.240
Oh, fuck! You need to go-
- Yeah, he's got blood

00:54:29.240 --> 00:54:30.080
all over the place.
- To the hospital.

00:54:30.080 --> 00:54:32.750
- I love the fact that
when he says expletives,

00:54:32.750 --> 00:54:33.640
he beeps it out,

00:54:33.640 --> 00:54:35.250
but you can still hear it.
- You still hear it.

00:54:35.250 --> 00:54:37.245
- He's making all these choices.

00:54:37.245 --> 00:54:38.385
- Hey, what's goin' on out there?

00:54:38.385 --> 00:54:39.530
- Now he comes out of the house.

00:54:39.530 --> 00:54:41.629
- A third version of himself.

00:54:41.629 --> 00:54:42.462
- Is he bleeding?

00:54:42.462 --> 00:54:44.149
Yeah, he's bleeding pretty good.

00:54:44.149 --> 00:54:45.860
What's he doing in the cornhole?

00:54:45.860 --> 00:54:47.197
He just wanted to do a little goof

00:54:47.197 --> 00:54:48.030
before he passed on.
- It's just such a strangely

00:54:48.030 --> 00:54:50.500
normal conversation.
- A little goof.

00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:51.450
- Pretty good, actually!

00:54:51.450 --> 00:54:53.950
Does he want a soda or anything?
I got some in the fridge.

00:54:53.950 --> 00:54:56.150
I'll ask him. Hey man, do you want a...

00:54:56.150 --> 00:54:58.380
- Oh no, he's dead.
- Aw, geez.

00:54:58.380 --> 00:54:59.220
What's up?

00:54:59.220 --> 00:55:00.471
I think he just passed away.

00:55:00.471 --> 00:55:01.330
- (chuckles) Passed away.

00:55:01.330 --> 00:55:02.163
- Yeah, he's passed on,

00:55:02.163 --> 00:55:03.670
unfortunately.
- (laughs) Passed on!

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:05.090
- Well, you know, cut-in-half
guys tend to do that.

00:55:05.090 --> 00:55:07.340
Yeah, half a guy doesn't tend
to last too long, does he?

00:55:07.340 --> 00:55:08.861
(Rhett laughing)
- That's the end.

00:55:08.861 --> 00:55:10.530
That's the end?
- So, just, that's it!

00:55:10.530 --> 00:55:12.750
- Yeah, "Half a guy doesn't
tend to last too long, does he?"

00:55:12.750 --> 00:55:14.170
I mean, where...

00:55:14.170 --> 00:55:15.970
Who comes up with this stuff?

00:55:15.970 --> 00:55:17.330
- Petey does.
- How does Petey

00:55:17.330 --> 00:55:18.530
come up with this stuff?

00:55:20.494 --> 00:55:23.650
It's brilliant! I mean, I can see this...

00:55:23.650 --> 00:55:25.930
If I met this guy and
he was totally normal,

00:55:25.930 --> 00:55:26.890
I would believe it.

00:55:26.890 --> 00:55:29.990
I mean, the performance
could be that masterful.

00:55:29.990 --> 00:55:31.250
He could be somewhere in the middle

00:55:31.250 --> 00:55:34.680
and there could be a lot of
strangeness in his real persona.

00:55:34.680 --> 00:55:37.380
But I can definitely see
him showing up in movies.

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:39.480
It has to already be happening.

00:55:39.480 --> 00:55:40.820
- Sure, yeah, sure.
- He has to be on sets-

00:55:40.820 --> 00:55:42.928
- He's going to auditions.
- Playing this character.

00:55:42.928 --> 00:55:44.374
- Let's watch another one.

00:55:44.374 --> 00:55:45.563
I don't even know which one this is.

00:55:45.563 --> 00:55:48.260
It just has 3 million
views, so it must be good.

00:55:48.260 --> 00:55:50.020
- Play. See?
(gentle music)

00:55:50.020 --> 00:55:53.010
- Okay, so he's watching-
- It's guys swimming.

00:55:53.010 --> 00:55:54.490
- He's watching guys swimming-
- WTF, this is sick.

00:55:54.490 --> 00:55:56.638
- In an Olympic pool, with himself.

00:55:56.638 --> 00:55:57.912
- Hey, what's up, guys?

00:55:57.912 --> 00:55:59.390
Hey, what's up?
- And then he comes in.

00:55:59.390 --> 00:56:00.770
- You got it, man.

00:56:00.770 --> 00:56:02.200
Epic, hold their breath?

00:56:02.200 --> 00:56:03.533
They hold their breath underwater

00:56:03.533 --> 00:56:04.897
(Rhett laughing)
and then they do one of these.

00:56:04.897 --> 00:56:05.747
What's goin' on?
- He's acting like a swimmer.

00:56:05.747 --> 00:56:06.930
Hey, man. Hey.

00:56:06.930 --> 00:56:08.240
Hey, man!
- None of these guys

00:56:08.240 --> 00:56:09.829
have ever seen swimmers.
- Guys swimming, yeah.

00:56:09.829 --> 00:56:10.910
Epic!
- Yeah.

00:56:10.910 --> 00:56:12.120
- Holding their breath?
- Yeah.

00:56:12.120 --> 00:56:14.050
They're fascinated by it.
- Doing one of these guys.

00:56:14.050 --> 00:56:16.940
No way!
- No way. Five of them.

00:56:16.940 --> 00:56:17.870
- It's guys swimming.

00:56:17.870 --> 00:56:18.703
Like fish?

00:56:18.703 --> 00:56:19.700
Well, yeah, but they're guys.

00:56:19.700 --> 00:56:20.533
Incredible.

00:56:20.533 --> 00:56:21.366
(Rhett laughing)
Guys swimming like fish.

00:56:21.366 --> 00:56:23.061
Guys swimming like fish.

00:56:23.061 --> 00:56:25.900
- (laughs) Now they're
slapping fives with each other.

00:56:25.900 --> 00:56:27.790
- I mean, the editing is so great,

00:56:27.790 --> 00:56:29.637
because it's just one guy!

00:56:29.637 --> 00:56:31.244
- And the music.

00:56:31.244 --> 00:56:34.020
- The music is strange.
(hands slapping)

00:56:34.020 --> 00:56:34.940
- Swimming guys.

00:56:34.940 --> 00:56:36.250
Exactly, man.

00:56:36.250 --> 00:56:37.660
- They're still giving fives.

00:56:37.660 --> 00:56:39.201
- It's like, what?

00:56:39.201 --> 00:56:40.034
- (laughs) I mean-
- And that's it.

00:56:40.034 --> 00:56:42.980
- It's just, I just-
- So, footage of swimmers-

00:56:42.980 --> 00:56:43.813
- It's just, they're watching-

00:56:43.813 --> 00:56:45.917
- Turns into-
- Two guys swimming.

00:56:45.917 --> 00:56:48.810
- "They" meaning him playing
five different characters-

00:56:48.810 --> 00:56:50.210
- Everybody in the same-
- Which, in the same...

00:56:50.210 --> 00:56:51.695
They're all the same guy-
- They're just dressed

00:56:51.695 --> 00:56:53.408
differently.
- In different clothes.

00:56:53.408 --> 00:56:54.360
(laughs) He's so great!

00:56:54.360 --> 00:56:56.860
- And he puts his music,
this like (hums weirdly),

00:56:58.820 --> 00:57:03.820
this weird ethereal music
underneath everything,

00:57:03.990 --> 00:57:07.690
that is also a very specific
and wonderful choice.

00:57:07.690 --> 00:57:09.440
- It's just so fresh.

00:57:09.440 --> 00:57:11.590
I think that's the thing
that I love about it

00:57:11.590 --> 00:57:14.720
is that I can't compare it to any other

00:57:14.720 --> 00:57:16.030
comedic sensibility.

00:57:16.030 --> 00:57:17.500
It's out of left field,

00:57:17.500 --> 00:57:22.500
so it surprises you and it
makes you feel a little envious.

00:57:23.110 --> 00:57:25.393
Like, I know that I could never, you know,

00:57:25.393 --> 00:57:27.260
I could never be that guy,

00:57:27.260 --> 00:57:29.820
but I'd like to have that ability.

00:57:29.820 --> 00:57:33.460
- Well, I think that,
yeah, we have a tendency...

00:57:34.490 --> 00:57:38.040
Again, I think that Petey is...

00:57:38.040 --> 00:57:39.410
This is calculated, of course.

00:57:39.410 --> 00:57:41.020
He knows why it's funny.

00:57:41.020 --> 00:57:44.323
He's self-aware enough
to know why he's funny,

00:57:47.148 --> 00:57:50.980
but you can tell, just
like you said earlier,

00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:52.107
if you met this dude,

00:57:52.107 --> 00:57:54.700
the way that he would
interact with you is like

00:57:54.700 --> 00:57:56.510
one of these characters-
- I don't know anymore.

00:57:56.510 --> 00:57:58.560
- No, what I'm saying is, like,

00:57:58.560 --> 00:58:00.530
I mean, that's his haircut.

00:58:00.530 --> 00:58:04.700
He has chosen to have that
long hair in that way, right?

00:58:04.700 --> 00:58:05.713
That's a choice.

00:58:06.784 --> 00:58:08.730
- You know, he could be in an
Allman Brothers cover band.

00:58:08.730 --> 00:58:10.030
- Right, but...

00:58:10.030 --> 00:58:12.110
And I think that there is
this, like, yeah, okay,

00:58:12.110 --> 00:58:14.830
well, we would have to
play some kind of character

00:58:14.830 --> 00:58:18.805
in order to achieve this, and
at that point, it would...

00:58:18.805 --> 00:58:19.980
This is one of the things that's happening

00:58:19.980 --> 00:58:21.500
with comedy in general, right?

00:58:21.500 --> 00:58:24.810
So there is this, in a good way,

00:58:24.810 --> 00:58:27.330
this new appreciation for authenticity.

00:58:27.330 --> 00:58:29.647
Again, he is playing a character

00:58:29.647 --> 00:58:31.110
and he's coming up with these situations,

00:58:31.110 --> 00:58:33.560
that, I'm not saying
that he would just bond

00:58:33.560 --> 00:58:35.640
with some dude over guys
swimming in that way.

00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:37.530
It's clearly a joke,
it's clearly elevated,

00:58:37.530 --> 00:58:39.510
and it's clearly scripted.

00:58:39.510 --> 00:58:40.930
But that thing you're talking about

00:58:40.930 --> 00:58:44.700
is this authenticity of...

00:58:44.700 --> 00:58:46.520
It's the same reason that people

00:58:46.520 --> 00:58:48.630
are so into commentary videos right now,

00:58:48.630 --> 00:58:52.370
is because I'm watching someone-

00:58:52.370 --> 00:58:54.780
- Honestly process-
- Process something,

00:58:54.780 --> 00:58:55.770
and if they're really funny

00:58:55.770 --> 00:58:58.273
and they make really funny
observations about it,

00:58:59.170 --> 00:59:01.560
they're kind of interacting with it

00:59:01.560 --> 00:59:04.340
in the way that I want to
interact with this stuff.

00:59:04.340 --> 00:59:07.240
- The best commentators are the ones

00:59:07.240 --> 00:59:10.160
that you never get a sense
they're trying too hard.

00:59:10.160 --> 00:59:13.830
That's absolutely something
that we have, you know,

00:59:13.830 --> 00:59:16.720
that we've had a problem with all along,

00:59:16.720 --> 00:59:20.320
is just trying too hard, pushing too hard.

00:59:20.320 --> 00:59:22.060
And you know, I think once we've...

00:59:22.060 --> 00:59:24.840
I mean, we're still relaxing
into "Good Mythical Morning,"

00:59:24.840 --> 00:59:27.970
and I'm encouraged, and I think
people are picking up on it,

00:59:27.970 --> 00:59:32.270
that we're just, we're pushing things

00:59:32.270 --> 00:59:34.177
less and less and just saying,

00:59:34.177 --> 00:59:36.277
"You know what, we're just going with it."

00:59:37.574 --> 00:59:39.650
And if we do something
where we are pushing

00:59:39.650 --> 00:59:40.850
the comedy of something,

00:59:41.870 --> 00:59:45.750
I think that you can
tell, or they can tell,

00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:48.960
that it's more from a place of, like,

00:59:48.960 --> 00:59:50.470
we're just motivated to wanna do it.

00:59:50.470 --> 00:59:52.390
We're not trying to achieve something

00:59:52.390 --> 00:59:54.190
or get something out of somebody.

00:59:54.190 --> 00:59:56.740
We think it's funny and so we're just

00:59:56.740 --> 00:59:58.330
screwing around with each other.

00:59:58.330 --> 01:00:00.580
I think that level of
authenticity is something,

01:00:00.580 --> 01:00:03.082
after all these years,
we're still working at,

01:00:03.082 --> 01:00:05.750
and still homing.

01:00:05.750 --> 01:00:07.230
You know, another-
- Well, no,

01:00:07.230 --> 01:00:08.100
that would be honing.

01:00:08.100 --> 01:00:09.430
That is the one...

01:00:09.430 --> 01:00:10.960
- (sighs) Oh, shit.
- You actually...

01:00:10.960 --> 01:00:13.427
Yeah, if you're still working
on it, you're still honing it.

01:00:13.427 --> 01:00:15.587
- I'm still working on
the use of that term.

01:00:15.587 --> 01:00:18.187
- You can just use honing
in all situations, though.

01:00:19.730 --> 01:00:21.280
- Well-
- That's what you concluded

01:00:21.280 --> 01:00:22.850
before, and then you just
went back on yourself.

01:00:22.850 --> 01:00:24.510
- It's your fault.
(Rhett chuckling)

01:00:24.510 --> 01:00:25.853
You get in my head, man!

01:00:26.840 --> 01:00:28.624
I mean-
- Still homing.

01:00:28.624 --> 01:00:31.550
- The other show that I
like, the HBO show "How To,"

01:00:31.550 --> 01:00:33.330
with John, what's his last name?

01:00:33.330 --> 01:00:34.950
- Yeah.
- I'd like to look it up.

01:00:34.950 --> 01:00:35.783
I can't remember

01:00:35.783 --> 01:00:36.903
his frickin' last name.
- I'll put it on my list of...

01:00:36.903 --> 01:00:40.560
We're having a creative
little meeting later today.

01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:42.983
- Yeah, it's on my list.
- It's on my list, yeah.

01:00:44.570 --> 01:00:45.790
Of favorite shows.

01:00:45.790 --> 01:00:47.170
- Look up his freaking last name,

01:00:47.170 --> 01:00:48.670
'cause as much as I love the show,

01:00:48.670 --> 01:00:49.660
I hate the fact that I can't

01:00:49.660 --> 01:00:50.616
remember right now.
- Well, I've got it.

01:00:50.616 --> 01:00:51.570
I've got it on my list.

01:00:51.570 --> 01:00:55.560
- Yeah, it's so, I mean,

01:00:55.560 --> 01:00:57.147
he films-
- Wilson, John Wilson.

01:00:57.147 --> 01:00:59.250
- "How To with John Wilson," he films...

01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:01.180
He's got reams of footage from his life

01:01:01.180 --> 01:01:03.610
living around New York City,

01:01:03.610 --> 01:01:06.560
and then he turns it into these...

01:01:06.560 --> 01:01:11.075
He pieces it together as
these narratives, kinda.

01:01:11.075 --> 01:01:13.747
There's your stories
that you're following.

01:01:13.747 --> 01:01:16.410
There's ideas that he's exploring,

01:01:16.410 --> 01:01:18.600
and there's lots of left turns.

01:01:18.600 --> 01:01:20.180
So it's not a beginning, middle, and end.

01:01:20.180 --> 01:01:22.000
It's a beginning, a
left turn, a left turn,

01:01:22.000 --> 01:01:25.400
look at where we ended up,
you loved every second of it.

01:01:25.400 --> 01:01:27.830
- But we have a difference
of opinion, I think,

01:01:27.830 --> 01:01:31.003
on just how authentic he is.

01:01:34.060 --> 01:01:38.320
How much is he the guy that
he is presenting himself,

01:01:38.320 --> 01:01:39.739
and how much is calculated?

01:01:39.739 --> 01:01:42.000
I think it's more calculated than you,

01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:43.193
last time we talked about it,

01:01:43.193 --> 01:01:44.140
at least.
- Yeah, I'm on the opposite

01:01:44.140 --> 01:01:47.560
side of Petey USA than you.
- But this is good,

01:01:47.560 --> 01:01:49.060
because this is exactly what...

01:01:49.060 --> 01:01:51.770
First of all, I don't
care how calculated it is,

01:01:51.770 --> 01:01:53.970
because the final product is believable.

01:01:53.970 --> 01:01:56.330
- Right.
- And that is how I think

01:01:56.330 --> 01:01:58.580
that all this move towards authenticity

01:01:58.580 --> 01:02:00.540
that's taking place in the creator-driven,

01:02:00.540 --> 01:02:04.270
entertainment system that is
TikTok, YouTube, whatever,

01:02:04.270 --> 01:02:08.354
it's not going to do
away with larger-budget,

01:02:08.354 --> 01:02:10.705
produced, scripted content.

01:02:10.705 --> 01:02:14.043
It just influences it, and
the way that it influences it

01:02:14.043 --> 01:02:16.300
is the stuff that we're talking about,

01:02:16.300 --> 01:02:20.190
which is really good...

01:02:20.190 --> 01:02:23.250
It's why comedians have
to be better actors

01:02:23.250 --> 01:02:25.240
than they had to be 20 years ago, I think,

01:02:25.240 --> 01:02:27.170
in order to make the kind
of comedy that I wanna see.

01:02:27.170 --> 01:02:32.170
And that is because you
need to be able to get me

01:02:32.190 --> 01:02:36.090
to buy into the authenticity, the reality,

01:02:36.090 --> 01:02:37.930
of the character that you're portraying.

01:02:37.930 --> 01:02:41.580
And then I can feel free to laugh.

01:02:41.580 --> 01:02:44.740
But the moment that I feel
like I sense the actor

01:02:44.740 --> 01:02:46.351
and I sense the writing,

01:02:46.351 --> 01:02:48.300
which is something that no one cared about

01:02:48.300 --> 01:02:51.200
when Chevy Chase was doing it in the '80s.

01:02:51.200 --> 01:02:54.930
And again, you can still go
back and enjoy all those things,

01:02:54.930 --> 01:02:57.980
legitimately enjoy
Chevy Chase in the '80s.

01:02:57.980 --> 01:02:59.720
I'm not saying it's not funny,

01:02:59.720 --> 01:03:02.220
but the reason that you
can't just do that now

01:03:02.220 --> 01:03:05.360
is 'cause people are
viewing all that old stuff

01:03:05.360 --> 01:03:08.070
through the lens of nostalgia
and you're appreciating it

01:03:08.070 --> 01:03:09.480
and you're giving it
the benefit of the doubt

01:03:09.480 --> 01:03:11.060
because of what it was.

01:03:11.060 --> 01:03:13.860
But nowadays, I do think the stuff that...

01:03:13.860 --> 01:03:15.520
I think that it isn't some grand

01:03:15.520 --> 01:03:17.050
calculation that we've made.

01:03:17.050 --> 01:03:19.330
I think that we're
responding to the environment

01:03:19.330 --> 01:03:21.270
of the comedy that we're
into and is around us.

01:03:21.270 --> 01:03:25.140
And we're like, this has gotta
be, it has to feel authentic.

01:03:25.140 --> 01:03:25.973
That doesn't mean that somebody

01:03:25.973 --> 01:03:27.270
is not gonna be a character actor.

01:03:27.270 --> 01:03:28.103
That doesn't mean that somebody

01:03:28.103 --> 01:03:29.700
is not gonna play a
completely different person.

01:03:29.700 --> 01:03:31.120
It doesn't mean that
somebody is going to be...

01:03:31.120 --> 01:03:32.650
You take Christopher Guest, I mean,

01:03:32.650 --> 01:03:35.480
one of the reasons that that
stuff holds up for me...

01:03:35.480 --> 01:03:37.920
We watched "Best in Show"-
- All the mockumentaries.

01:03:37.920 --> 01:03:40.580
- We watched "Best in Show"
a couple of weeks ago,

01:03:40.580 --> 01:03:42.523
me and Jesse and Shepherd, and
Shepherd absolutely loved it.

01:03:42.523 --> 01:03:44.930
And you know, you take the character

01:03:44.930 --> 01:03:46.710
that Christopher Guest
plays in that movie,

01:03:46.710 --> 01:03:48.860
which is (laughs) obviously-
- The hound dog guy?

01:03:48.860 --> 01:03:49.723
- The hound, it's not him,

01:03:49.723 --> 01:03:52.530
it's this dude from some
place in North Carolina,

01:03:52.530 --> 01:03:54.110
but I can't remember, I
think it was a made-up name.

01:03:54.110 --> 01:03:55.843
Pine Tree? Pine Nut, North Carolina.

01:03:55.843 --> 01:03:57.680
- Yeah. (laughs)

01:03:57.680 --> 01:03:59.110
- But everyone plays-
- That's so good.

01:03:59.110 --> 01:03:59.943
- Those characters

01:03:59.943 --> 01:04:02.120
so authentically-
- What does he list out?

01:04:02.120 --> 01:04:03.340
He lists out different types of-

01:04:03.340 --> 01:04:04.600
- Types of nuts.

01:04:04.600 --> 01:04:05.450
- Types of nuts.

01:04:05.450 --> 01:04:07.718
- You got pine nuts, you got peanuts,

01:04:07.718 --> 01:04:10.168
you got almonds nuts, you got...

01:04:10.168 --> 01:04:11.760
- And he loves those dogs.

01:04:11.760 --> 01:04:14.820
- Yeah, but the reason that he's funny-

01:04:14.820 --> 01:04:17.140
- Is 'cause you can tell
it's Christopher Guest

01:04:17.140 --> 01:04:18.710
doing this off the top of his head.

01:04:18.710 --> 01:04:20.420
- But he's also tapping into something

01:04:20.420 --> 01:04:24.290
that is representative of
something that is real and exists.

01:04:24.290 --> 01:04:27.660
- Yeah, which is why "Talladega Nights"

01:04:27.660 --> 01:04:31.730
is kinda like the outlier
Will Ferrell movie for me,

01:04:31.730 --> 01:04:33.690
because I knew how true it was.

01:04:33.690 --> 01:04:35.910
And it was actually a lot
more grounded than someone

01:04:35.910 --> 01:04:39.674
who didn't live, you know,
grow up around where we did,

01:04:39.674 --> 01:04:42.190
would think that it was
just too ridiculous,

01:04:42.190 --> 01:04:43.777
but it was actually more grounded.

01:04:43.777 --> 01:04:47.447
- Truth, I mean, there's
all kinds of theories

01:04:47.447 --> 01:04:49.249
about what makes something funny,

01:04:49.249 --> 01:04:51.739
and philosophers have
been debating it forever,

01:04:51.739 --> 01:04:54.950
but I always kinda
gravitate towards this idea

01:04:54.950 --> 01:04:56.076
that the truth...

01:04:56.076 --> 01:04:57.813
There's truth in comedy,

01:04:57.813 --> 01:05:00.088
and that's the thing that
you're kinda connecting with.

01:05:00.088 --> 01:05:02.890
And you can get to truth through things

01:05:02.890 --> 01:05:04.920
that are absurd and completely unreal,

01:05:04.920 --> 01:05:06.790
like we both are huge fans
of "The Mighty Boosh,"

01:05:06.790 --> 01:05:10.420
and nothing that happens
in "The Mighty Boosh" is...

01:05:10.420 --> 01:05:12.519
It's almost like you can't...

01:05:12.519 --> 01:05:14.522
You have to pick a side
of the argument, right?

01:05:14.522 --> 01:05:17.250
You have to be like, I'm
going to try to be authentic

01:05:17.250 --> 01:05:20.860
and represent the truth in a
way that you'll find relatable,

01:05:20.860 --> 01:05:23.820
or I'm gonna be so far
removed from your own

01:05:23.820 --> 01:05:25.756
understanding and
experience that you'll be...

01:05:25.756 --> 01:05:27.825
It's almost, there's an uncanny valley,

01:05:27.825 --> 01:05:31.450
I think, between absurdist and realist,

01:05:31.450 --> 01:05:32.460
that I just don't...
- Yeah, like-

01:05:32.460 --> 01:05:34.368
- If you live in the uncanny valley,

01:05:34.368 --> 01:05:35.201
you can't get into it.
- I love "Rick and Morty."

01:05:35.201 --> 01:05:37.670
I mean, the absurdist nature
of "Rick and Morty," I mean,

01:05:37.670 --> 01:05:40.630
as an animated show, I guess,
as an old fart like me,

01:05:40.630 --> 01:05:43.971
it's like you have that suspension
of disbelief, and like...

01:05:43.971 --> 01:05:46.300
But yeah, "The Mighty Boosh" did that too,

01:05:46.300 --> 01:05:49.450
where it was like, you're
just gonna embrace the crazy.

01:05:49.450 --> 01:05:53.000
When you talk about the
evolution of comedy,

01:05:53.000 --> 01:05:55.790
and of television, I mean,
it does make me think

01:05:55.790 --> 01:05:57.640
of like the brilliance of "WandaVision,"

01:05:57.640 --> 01:06:02.640
and how I noticed that I enjoyed

01:06:02.670 --> 01:06:07.670
the later episodes better
because they were...

01:06:07.760 --> 01:06:11.610
It was easier to enjoy them
because the form was something

01:06:11.610 --> 01:06:14.010
that was closer to-
- It's more familiar.

01:06:14.010 --> 01:06:16.315
- It was more, well, it was more-

01:06:16.315 --> 01:06:17.520
- [Both] Real.

01:06:17.520 --> 01:06:20.520
- Because of that trajectory

01:06:20.520 --> 01:06:21.840
that you're describing in comedy

01:06:21.840 --> 01:06:23.600
applies to television in general.

01:06:23.600 --> 01:06:25.558
You know, I think about,
if you go back to the...

01:06:25.558 --> 01:06:29.180
If you showed, just to, even...

01:06:29.180 --> 01:06:31.250
If you showed an episode
of "Modern Family,"

01:06:31.250 --> 01:06:34.058
which is still very heightened, you know,

01:06:34.058 --> 01:06:35.560
but if you go back and you show that

01:06:35.560 --> 01:06:38.680
to someone from the 1950s,

01:06:38.680 --> 01:06:42.880
and you remove the shock
of technology and culture,

01:06:42.880 --> 01:06:46.390
and you just made it about
like the way they were acting?

01:06:46.390 --> 01:06:49.784
By the way, I think that's one of the...

01:06:49.784 --> 01:06:52.400
"WandaVision" was just a
ballsy move, what they did,

01:06:52.400 --> 01:06:55.660
and like I told you, I
think that going back

01:06:55.660 --> 01:06:58.410
and watching it just to
appreciate the acting

01:06:58.410 --> 01:07:01.001
and how they acted within
the different eras,

01:07:01.001 --> 01:07:02.819
was pretty brilliant.

01:07:02.819 --> 01:07:04.563
- And especially-
- But I don't know how-

01:07:04.563 --> 01:07:06.863
